<Base title="About" person description="Brandon Freitag is a strategy and execution executive based in Denver and Phoenix, formerly at Google Cloud and DaVita.">
  <p class="kicker">Both sides of the table</p>
  <h1>About</h1>

  <img src="/brandon.jpg" alt="Brandon Freitag" class="headshot" width="200" height="250" />

  <p>Most of Brandon Freitag's career has been spent close to the failure side of a specific problem: the gap between what organizations intend to achieve and what gets delivered. He has stood inside that gap with direct accountability for the outcome, not at a distance from it.</p>

  <p>His career began on the manufacturing floor, where failure was countable scrap and measurement settled arguments. That discipline earned him <a href="https://patents.google.com/patent/US5553538A" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">a solo patent</a> and a Six Sigma Black Belt, and a question he has carried ever since: why do some changes hold while others quietly fail?</p>

  <figure class="patent">
    <p class="patent__label">U.S. Patent 5,553,538 &middot; Sep. 10, 1996</p>
    <img src="/patent-5553538.png" alt="The first drawings sheet of U.S. Patent 5,553,538, showing figures 1 and 2 of the stencil printing method" width="1200" height="832" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="patent__caption">
      From the drawings of <a href="https://patents.google.com/patent/US5553538A" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Method and apparatus for stencil printing printed circuit boards</a>, sole inventor Brandon R. Freitag, assigned to Motorola. The figures on this site are drawn the way this sheet taught him to draw them.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>From there it moved across two distinct vantage points. For most of it, he worked inside cloud and enterprise technology companies, helping organizations build the case for large-scale change and then supporting the work that followed. Later, he moved to the other side of that equation, serving as an executive at a Fortune 200 company where he owned the outcome directly. The view from inside is different. The failures he had watched from a distance were now his to explain and address.</p>

  <p>The scope of that work has been significant. At Google Cloud, he built and led a national strategic architecture practice engaging on some of the company's most complex and strategic pursuits, sustaining a win rate twice the field baseline and influencing several billion dollars in total contract value. At DaVita, leading a globally distributed organization of up to 140, he reduced time-to-environment for a mission-critical patient-facing system by 90% and drove a 27% operating cost reduction through enterprise-wide cloud adoption, in a highly regulated environment, against compliance and delivery commitments that could not move.</p>

  <p>What he kept finding, from both positions, is that the failures are not random. The same patterns appear across every kind of organizational change, they surface early, and they can be diagnosed long before they become costly.</p>

  <p>That observation is the foundation of Work That Holds, his essays on why organizational change holds or doesn't. Its core series, <em>Why Change Fails</em>, is six papers on the structural reasons large-scale initiatives break down and the disciplines required to address them before they do.</p>

  <p>His book, <a href="https://builttobereplaced.com"><em>Built to Be Replaced: How Solution Engineers Outlast AI</em></a>, publishes in January 2027.</p>

  <p>That body of work is now a practice: <a href="https://worththatholds.com">Worth That Holds</a>, where he advises leaders on organizational and AI readiness.</p>

  <p>Brandon is based in Denver, Colorado and Phoenix, Arizona.</p>

  <p><a href="https://linkedin.com/in/brfreitag" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Connect on LinkedIn</a></p>
